## A spotlight on the past

A model uses <GlossaryPopover term="attention">attention</GlossaryPopover> to let each token look
back over everything before it and pull in what's useful. Picture each token holding a spotlight. It
shines on the earlier tokens, brightly on the relevant ones and dimly on the rest, and takes in a
blend of whatever it lights up. Attention is the core idea of the <GlossaryPopover term="transformer">transformer</GlossaryPopover>, the design introduced in 2017
that almost every modern LLM uses, GPT-2 included.<Cite id="vaswani2017" />

## Queries, keys, and values

<At levels="simple">

How does a token decide where to shine? Each token makes three things from its numbers, using
weights learned in training: a **query** (what it's looking for), a **key** (what it has to offer),
and a **value** (what it passes on). Where one token's query matches another token's key well, the
spotlight shines brighter. Each token then takes in a blend of the values, mostly from the tokens
it lights up most.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

How does a token decide where to shine? Each token's vector is turned into three new vectors, using
weights learned in training:

- a **query**: what this token is looking for,
- a **key**: what this token has to offer, to anyone looking,
- a **value**: what this token passes on if it gets picked.

To score how relevant one token is to another, multiply the first token's query with the second
token's key, number by number, and add up the results. When the two vectors point in similar
directions (think of the nearby words in <ChapterRef to="embeddings" />), the score is high. The scores are divided by a fixed number
to keep them from getting too large, then softmax (<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />) turns them into percentages. Finally each
token takes a weighted blend of the values.

</At>

Step through it with made-up numbers:

<QKVExplainer />

Notice the blank cells: a token can't look at tokens that come after it. When the model is
predicting the next word, those words don't exist yet.

<At levels="deep">

### The formula

Written out for every token at once, one attention head computes

$$
\operatorname{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \operatorname{softmax}\!\left(\frac{Q K^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_k}} + M\right) V
$$

Each row of $Q$, $K$, and $V$ is one token's query, key, or value. They come from the token vectors
$X$, one row per token, times three learned weight matrices: $Q = X W_Q$, $K = X W_K$, and
$V = X W_V$. $Q K^{\top}$ is the grid of scores, every query times every key. $d_k$ is the number
of values in each key, and $\sqrt{d_k}$ is the fixed number the scores are divided by. $M$ is the
mask: 0 where a token may look, and $-\infty$ where it would look ahead, which softmax turns into a
weight of exactly 0. Softmax runs along each row, so each token's weights add up to 1.<Cite id="vaswani2017" />

In GPT-2, each token vector has 768 numbers and each layer has 12 heads, so each head works with
$d_k = 768 / 12 = 64$ numbers, and the scores are divided by $\sqrt{64} = 8$. The 12 heads' outputs
are put side by side, back to 768 numbers, and multiplied by one more learned matrix,
$W_O$.<Cite id="vaswani2017" /> Unlike the toy, GPT-2 also normalizes the token vectors before they
go in (more in the next chapter).<Cite id="radford2019" />

The grid has a cell for every pair of tokens, so it grows with the square of the text's length.
With the mask, $n$ tokens need

$$
\frac{n(n+1)}{2}
$$

scores in each head: 524,800 for a full window of 1,024 tokens, in each of GPT-2's 144 heads.

</At>

## Which word came first?

<At levels="simple">

Attention on its own has a blind spot: nothing in a score says where a token sits. "The dog
chased the cat." and "The cat chased the dog." contain the same words, so attention would blend
them the same way, and who chased whom would be lost. So GPT-2 adds a second list of numbers to
each token, a <GlossaryPopover term="position-vector">position vector</GlossaryPopover>, that says
where it is: first, second, third, and so on, up to 1,024 places.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Look again at how a score is made: one token's query times another token's key. Nothing in that
says where either token sits in the text. Take "the dog chased the cat." and "the cat chased the
dog." At the final ".", one attention step would see the same six tokens in both, just in another
order, and blend them in exactly the same way. Who chased whom would be lost.

So before the first layer, GPT-2 adds a second vector to each token's vector: a <GlossaryPopover term="position-vector">position vector</GlossaryPopover>,
one for each place in the text, learned in training like the token vectors. (This is position in
the text, not the position in meaning from <ChapterRef to="embeddings" />.) Now "dog" second and
"dog" fifth start out as different vectors, and attention can tell the sentences apart. GPT-2 has
1,024 position vectors, one for each place in its context window (<ChapterRef to="the-context-problem" />).
There is no vector for place 1,025, which is one reason the window stops there.

Newer models such as Llama 3 add no position vectors. Instead they turn each query and key by an
angle that grows with its place in the text, so the score between two tokens also depends on how
far apart they are.<Cite id="su2024" /><Cite id="grattafiori2024" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

In symbols, GPT-2's first layer starts each token from

$$
x_i = e_{t_i} + p_i
$$

where $i$ is the token's place in the text, $t_i$ is its token ID, $e_{t_i}$ is that token's row in
the 50,257 × 768 table of token vectors, and $p_i$ is row $i$ of the 1,024 × 768 table of position
vectors.

Rotary position embedding, the method Llama 3 uses, works on the queries and keys instead. It
splits each one into pairs of numbers and turns pair $j$ of the token in place $m$ by the angle
$m\,\theta_j$, with

$$
\theta_j = b^{-2j/d}
$$

where $d$ is the length of the query and $b$ is a fixed base. When the query in place $m$ meets the
key in place $n$, the two turns partly cancel, and the score depends on the places only through the
distance between them, $m - n$.<Cite id="su2024" /> The original paper used $b = 10{,}000$; Llama 3
raised it to 500,000 to handle longer texts.<Cite id="grattafiori2024" />

</At>

## Real attention in GPT-2

Here are attention patterns recorded from GPT-2. Each line runs from the word doing the looking
(right) to an earlier word (left); thicker lines mean more attention.

<AttentionViewer />

Some patterns are surprisingly readable:

- **Who gets the drink?** In "When Mary and John went to the store, John gave a drink to", GPT-2
  predicts " Mary" next. Layer 9, head 9 looks from the last word back at "Mary" far more than at
  either "John" (the pull toward "When" is explained below).
  Researchers who took this behavior apart found a small set of heads, including 9.6 and 9.9, that
  find the right name and copy it forward.<Cite id="wang2022" />
- **Whose fleece?** In the nursery rhyme, layer 9, head 0 looks from "its" back to "lamb".
- **What was too big?** The same head looks from "it" mostly to "suitcase", in *both* versions of
  the trophy sentence. That isn't a mistake in the viewer: "big" and "small" come after "it", and a
  word can only look back. The model has to sort out "it" later, at the words after the clue.

<At levels="simple">

GPT-2 isn't very good at that last kind of puzzle. The patterns you can read are a few bright
spots in a mostly murky picture.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

GPT-2 isn't very good at that last kind of puzzle. Even the largest GPT-2 answered 70.70% of
Winograd questions correctly, where guessing gets 50%.<Cite id="radford2019" /> The patterns you can
read are a few bright spots in a mostly murky picture.

</At>

## Many heads, many layers

<At levels="simple">

One set of queries, keys, and values is called an <GlossaryPopover term="attention-head">attention head</GlossaryPopover>.
GPT-2 runs 12 heads side by side in each of its 12 layers, each free to look for something
different. Switch heads in the viewer: many don't have a job you can name, and many put most of
their attention on the very first word, a resting spot for when there's nothing useful to
find.<Cite id="xiao2024" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

One set of queries, keys, and values is called an <GlossaryPopover term="attention-head">attention head</GlossaryPopover>. Each layer of GPT-2 runs
12 heads side by side, each with its own weights, so each can look for something different. With
12 layers, that's 144 heads. Layers and heads are numbered from 0, as in research papers.

Switch to other heads in the viewer and you'll see that many don't have a job you can name. You'll
also see lots of heads putting most of their attention on the very first word. In these sentences,
from the fourth word on, that happens more than half the time. Researchers think it's a resting
spot: softmax always has to share out 100%, so a head with nothing useful to find parks its
attention there.<Cite id="xiao2024" />

</At>

<At levels="simple">

Here is one head in full, for the sentence about Mary and John. Each row is a word looking back,
each column a word it could look at. Step through what the head works out: a score for every pair,
then the mask that hides every later word, then softmax, which turns each row into percentages.
The buttons page through all 144 heads.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Here is one head in full, for the sentence about Mary and John: every row is a word doing the
looking, every column a word it could look at. Step through the formula from the queries and keys
above: first the score for every pair, then the mask, then softmax along each row. Page through all
144 heads with the buttons.

</At>

<AttentionGrid />

<At levels="simple">

The head works out a score for every pair, even for words that come later; the mask then throws
those away, 91 of the 196 squares. For head 9.9, the last word's scores for "Mary" and "John" are
close, 4.25 and 3.23, yet after softmax "Mary" gets 36% and "John" 13%. Softmax turns a small lead
in score into a big lead in weight.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The scores exist for every pair, later words included: the head computes them all at once, and the
mask throws away the 91 of 196 that look ahead before softmax sees them. For head 9.9, the last
word's scores for "Mary" and the first "John" differ by about 1 (4.25 against 3.23), yet after
softmax "Mary" gets 36% and that "John" 13%. Softmax turns small gaps in score into large gaps in
weight. "When" scores 4.24 and also gets 36%: the resting spot described above.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

Since a weight is $e^{s}$ divided by the row's total, two scores that differ by $\Delta$ get
weights in the ratio $e^{\Delta}$, whatever the rest of the row holds: $e^{4.25 - 3.23} \approx 2.8$,
and 36% is about 2.8 times 13%.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- Attention lets each token pull in information from any earlier token, weighted by relevance.
- Each token makes a query, a key, and a value. Query times key gives a score; softmax turns scores
  into weights; the weights blend the values.
- A token can only look back, never ahead.
- Attention alone ignores word order, so GPT-2 adds a learned position vector to each token's
  vector.
- GPT-2 has 12 heads in each of its 12 layers. A few have clear jobs, like finding the name that
  comes next; most are harder to read.

<At levels="deep">

- One head computes $\operatorname{softmax}(Q K^{\top} / \sqrt{d_k} + M)\,V$. In GPT-2, $d_k = 64$.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** attention moves information between tokens. How does it fit into a whole model?
